// expect-exit: 0
//
// THE CONSUMER. A program that imports `ntime` the way an application will,
// and does nothing else.
//
// WHAT IT ASSERTS, AND IT IS NOT "the library works". It asserts that the
// PUBLIC SURFACE IS IMPORTABLE. It was written when the surface was EMPTY --
// importing an empty surface was exactly the test -- and at 0.0.4 the surface
// became 35 names from `src/core/`: this file goes on compiling as names are
// added to `src/lib.npk`, and the day it does NOT, the surface changed in a
// way that breaks every consumer -- which is a MAJOR version by TM-013 and had
// better be a deliberate act rather than a discovery.
//
// THE IMPORT IS A RELATIVE PATH, AND THAT IS A DEFECT WE ARE LIVING WITH
// (0.0.1 P-9, `BUILD.md` B-16). The shape an application SHOULD write is
//
//     use "ntime/lib.npk".*;
//
// the dependency-root form. It does not resolve: the loader's dependency-root
// list (`RootList`) is created empty in the compiler's driver and nothing ever
// adds to it, so only `./` and `../` paths work. That is `meta/OPEN_QUESTIONS.md`
// O-N1, and this comment is here so that the day O-N1 closes, the thing to
// change is greppable -- `git grep -n 'O-N1' tests/` finds it.
mod:import;

// The umbrella, by relative path. `.*` is right HERE and wrong in
// `src/lib.npk`: a consumer wants the whole published surface in one line,
// which is the entire reason the umbrella exists, while the umbrella itself
// must name every export deliberately (P-8).
use "../../src/lib.npk".*;

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    // Nothing, on purpose -- and specifically no arithmetic. A single
    // `i + 1i64` here would arm `IntOverflow` and this file would stop being
    // a measurement of what THE IMPORT costs (`SAFETY.md` S-4b, TM-107).
    exit 0i32;
};

// THE MOVE PREDICTED BY THIS COMMENT HAS HAPPENED, AT CYCLE 0.0.4, AND THE
// PREVIOUS TEXT IS KEPT SO THE BEFORE-AND-AFTER IS ONE READ.
//
// It used to say: *"The four unconditional floor arms and the wildcard, which
// is `tests/probe/probe11d_floor_only.npk`'s set exactly. That this compiles
// UNCHANGED is the measurement: importing `ntime`'s umbrella today costs a
// consumer NOTHING beyond the floor. The number is expected to move as modules
// gain `error:` declarations and arithmetic -- and this file is where the move
// will first show."*
//
// IT SHOWED HERE FIRST, EXACTLY AS WRITTEN. `src/core/` landed, the umbrella
// went from re-exporting nothing to re-exporting 35 names, and this file was
// the only unit in the suite to go red -- `NITPICK-REACH-002` naming four arms
// by hand. **The bill for importing `ntime`'s public surface is now EIGHT:**
//
//     8 = 4 (S-4b's floor: HeapBadRequest, HeapOom, Unreachable, WildLeak)
//       + 1 IntOverflow   -- `n * #size_of<T>()` in `vec_init`, `bytes_reserve`
//       + 1 OutOfBounds   -- the TM-129 index guards in both accessors
//       + 1 DivByZero     -- `put_uint`'s `% 10` and `/ 10`
//       + 1 DivOverflow   -- the same two operators
//
// STILL ZERO `error:` DECLARATIONS: all four additions are ARITHMETIC and
// INDEXING, not identities. `check_error_budget` reports 0 of 3 declared, and
// the budget is untouched. That is TM-107 exactly -- an import's arm bill is
// its `fail` sites PLUS its arithmetic, charged per MODULE -- and it is why
// this file still contains no arithmetic of its own: every one of these four
// was bought by the IMPORT.
//
// AND `DivByZero`/`DivOverflow` ARE FOR TRAPS THAT CANNOT FIRE HERE: the only
// divisor in `src/core/` is the literal `10`. The bill is what the front end
// thinks can reach the handler, not what can. The same gap, in the other
// direction, is what `SAFETY.md` S-17c records about `OutOfBounds`.
func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (DivByZero)      { exit 97i32; },
        (DivOverflow)    { exit 98i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
